{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Plan-review run in this environment: **BLOCK**, 2 blocking findings. Both are architectural and neither was anticipated by the original plan. Recording them because they are load-bearing for the epic, not just this story.\n\n## Blocking 1 — the transitive `mistune` dependency vs the stdlib-only engine\nmarklas is mistune-based. The plan vendors only `src/rebar/_vendor/marklas/` and never says how mistune is satisfied. But the reconciler engine that imports `adf.py` is contractually dependency-free: `pyproject.toml:62-66` keeps core `dependencies` at pyyaml/jsonschema/referencing only, and `pyproject.toml:69` states the engine assets \"are stdlib-only and ship as package data under `rebar/_engine/`\". If vendored marklas imports mistune at runtime, `adf.markdown_to_adf` raises ImportError in a plain install and the stdlib-only contract (guarded by `test_core_optionality.py`) breaks.\n\n## Blocking 2 — `src/rebar/_vendor/` is not on the reconciler subprocess's import path\n`adf.py` is imported as `from rebar_reconciler.adapters.jira import adf` (`rich_text_codec.py:19`) — a package shipped as subprocess package data under `src/rebar/_engine/`. An out-of-tree `src/rebar/_vendor/` path is not established to be importable from that subprocess, and the plan does not say how `adf.py` resolves the import.\n\n## Supporting measurements\n- No vendoring precedent exists anywhere under `src/rebar/_engine/` (searched); `src/rebar/_vendor/` does not exist either.\n- Vendoring both libraries means ~398 KB across 56 Python files (marklas 18 files / 204 KB; mistune 38 files / 194 KB) placed inside a tree the project documents as stdlib-only.\n\n## The open design question (needs a decision before this story is claimable)\nThree candidate resolutions, none of which the ticket currently authorizes:\n1. **Vendor both** marklas + mistune under a path that ships as engine package data (e.g. `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_vendor/`), recording both shas + LICENSEs. Keeps `import adf` working everywhere; puts 398 KB of third-party code inside the stdlib-only tree and redefines that contract.\n2. **Lazy-import inside the three functions**, routing ImportError to the existing plain-text `adf.py` fallback, and ship marklas+mistune as a pinned OPTIONAL extra rather than vendored source. Preserves the stdlib-only contract and `import adf` stays clean; the Markdown-aware path is then unavailable in a no-extras install, which story 3388's cutover must tolerate.\n3. **Implement the encode in-house** in `adf.py`. The prior plan rejected this on evidence (a hand-rolled attempt reached only ~90.7% fixed point), so it is recorded here only for completeness.\n\nOption 2 looks most consistent with the repo's existing seams (it mirrors how `agents`/`metrics`/`grounding` extras degrade), but it changes the epic's stated architecture, which pins `src/rebar/_vendor/marklas/` in the signed epic plan — so it is an operator/driver decision, not one to take unilaterally.\n\n## Other measurements from this review cycle (see also the premise-correction comment above)\nIdempotence reproduces (99.75% measured over a 400-body random sample of 4,996 live bodies, 0 conversion errors). Structural richness does NOT reproduce: 22.8% measured vs 56.7% claimed. The combined-mark oscillation is confirmed as an exact 2-cycle (`A **~~both~~** end` <-> `A ~~**both**~~ end`), so the mark-order canonicalizer is required. marklas also DROPS HTML comments, which would remove rebar's own `<!-- rebar:reconciler-echo -->` marker."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786571245744070001,"uuid":"9920b94b-6ddf-4571-98f5-352e47adb52a"}